# Dart File Headers

## Requirement
Every Dart file under `lib/` **must** start with an **explanatory** file-purpose header so a reader immediately understands:
1. **What kind of file** this is (shell tab page, pushed page, repository, widget, theme, etc.)
2. **Where it sits** in SoilGood (e.g. content area of the app shell, outside the shell, data layer only)
3. **What it is for** / who uses it

Do **not** write vague one-liners like “Home page” or “Auth helpers.” Write like you are explaining to a teammate who opens the file cold.

## Format
`///` doc comment first, then `library;` (required so the analyzer does not report `dangling_library_doc_comments`):

```dart
/// Explanatory first sentence: kind of file + role in the app.
///
/// Second paragraph: where it appears / who calls it / what data it owns.
/// Keep farmer/product context when useful.
library;

import '...';
```

## Rules
- Prefer **2–4 short sentences** (can be 2–3 `///` paragraphs). Still readable — not an essay.
- Say **where UI lives**: shell content area, pushed route outside shell, auth/onboarding before shell, shared chrome, etc.
- Say **layer** when not UI: data/repository, controller/logic, model, service, theme/config.
- Always include `library;` right after the header.
- Class/function docs stay separate and shorter; do not dump the whole API in the file header.
- New Dart file → write this header **first**. Missing header on edit → add it.

## Good example

```dart
/// Home feature page shown inside the app shell content area (first bottom-nav tab).
///
/// This is what the farmer sees after login/onboarding: live soil readings from
/// Supabase plus the Open-Meteo forecast for their saved farm pin. Not a pushed
/// route — the bottom nav stays mounted while this page is visible.
library;
```

## Bad example (too thin)

```dart
/// Home dashboard — live soil + weather.
library;
```

## Also required
Short comments on non-trivial functions remain required (see `coding-standards.mdc`).
